I am very pleased that John White has  agreed to talk with me today about our  accountability system in the United  States and the various efforts to raise  standards through common core and  through many other actions taken at  state levels John White is the state  superintendent for education for the  state of Louisiana and was also deputy  Chancellor in New York City so it has a  breadth of knowledge about how the  intergovernmental system needs to work  and it's very especially interesting to  talk with somebody who has been so  successful in Louisiana at raising  student performance from what was  seconds at the bottom to the most  rapidly improving state in the United  States under his leadership  so John it's great to have you with me  today great event and I would like to  ask you is testing an important part of  your thinking is holding schools  accountable an important part of your  thinking about how you get gains in  student performance and a more effective  educational system it is and no question  that measurement is has to be an  important component of any plan for  success there's a lot of ways we can  improve testing a lot of ways that we  can improve our accountability systems  the every student succeeds act I think  at least calls us to can seriously  consider those questions in the States  there are some serious risks involved in  all of this too but no question if you  the step to begin to measure learning is  an important one it can be done better  it can be done more efficiently but it's  a critical component as a starting point  of any plan for change well just a lot  of people were on board with this idea  back in 2002 when No Child Left Behind  was introduced into Congress and a  bipartisan coalition Democrats and  Republicans alike supported aspire  helming margin but as time went on a lot  of opposition developed to the testing  and the holding schools accountable that  was required by No Child Left Behind  what did they do wrong or why did that  political opposition develop well I  think there's a difference between what  was done wrong and what why the  political opposition develops but I  think that in terms of what was done  wrong the president has talked about  this there it has spawned an industry no  question about that and and I'm not sure  if every element of that industry is  what we want we don't need our kids  taking tests every day multiple times  over the course of a day it should be a  checkup and that's what it is and it's  fair to say that we or the establishment  or industry have not effectively  communicated to parents this is how this  benefits you this is not just a crusade  to benefit the disenfranchised this is  for everybody this is beneficial and we  haven't done as much as we needed to to  communicate that but one of the mistakes  that I thought was made by No Child Left  Behind was the adequate yearly progress  way of defining what was a good school  instead of looking at how much kids are  learning in the course of one year how  much more do they know this year than  they did one year ago which I would have  thought would have been the logical way  of trying to find out of the school's  effective or not the good news is people  I think discovered that you know quickly  years into No Child Left Behind that the  idea of just how many students are at a  certain level in a certain school is not  really an effective way of measuring the  effect of that school you know in our  state for example if you look at the a  schools the schools that are rated a  thirty percent of its mean in some  schools 90 percent of struggling  students in those schools are hitting  the mark in some state in some of those  a rated schools only thirty percent of  the struggling students are hitting the  mark so you've got wide wide differences  when you really dig into the data behind  what is in an a school so you will give  an A to a school where the students are  performing at a fairly low level if  they're showing substantial improvement  from one year to this well I think we  should do that I think right now what we  do and I probably didn't explain it as  well as I should have but what we do is  we give aid to schools that are making  great strides with kids we also give  aids to some schools that aren't making  great strides but just have  high-performing kids to begin with and  there's obviously a distinction  they're in a school can be one where  kids are just performing at a high level  but it can also be one where kids came  performing at a deplorable low level and  then really made games that's an A also  and we need to redefine in our state and  in many states what constitutes in a  school now the state school chief  officers I've forgotten exactly how that  name goes but and the governor's the  National Governors Association agreed to  put into place Common Core State  Standards that was backed by the Obama  administration with its race to the top  and initiative and its waiver policies  so that states didn't have to abide by  some of those NCLB requirements if in  fact they would adopt the Common Core  State Standards and make other reforms  as well so once again this this at the  beginning had widespread support and  well that's first of all talk about that  widespread support do do you agree that  it was there at the beginning I do and  the most simple way that I can I can  rationalize it to my in my mind and for  those that I work with is we have  historically been the 48th or 49th  ranked state in the country and  academics we have historically been in  the top five in football the reason  we're in the top five in football is  because we're willing to go to any state  and we're willing to compete but in  academics it's always just been well how  did one town in Louisiana do against  another town we can't get to the top in  a valid way unless we're willing to go  to any state and put our education  system up against it that's how you grow  that's how you progress is you're  willing to open your doors and to  compete so the principles of success on  the football field are principles of  success in our academic system if we  don't have something that we share with  other states the ability to measure  ourselves against everybody else there's  not really a valid platform for how we  need to progress but a lot of people  feel like okay the history of education  in the South in general Louisiana  included is very different than the  history of Education in the Northeast  where you've had back to the days of the  pilgrims a real commitment to teaching  children early on  is it fair to put them all on the same  platform and say you've got to be  compared to those people in other parts  of the country not just your own well  date you know there's a difference  between what's fair to the adults and  what's fair to the students and you need  to be fair to both we have 300 years of  subjugation of African American people  in our state we are an agrarian state  that is coming into a 21st century  economy and natural resources state I'm  not going to argue that that's a  different that that's the same  conditions in which Massachusetts or New  York or state in the Northeast operates  in clearly we come into this from a  different perspective but what is fair  to the kids who are inheriting in the  year 2016 a multinational seriously  competitive economy is to have  expectations that are those that the  workforce will have for them which is  you should be able to work and live and  succeed anywhere what's there to the  adults is not to demoralize everybody  just because you have those high  expectations and you can do both of  those things you can have a system that  has the highest expectations possible  for children while having a rational  system of improvement for the adults who  operate the system so the common core  standards principle is one that you  endorse you continue to endorse despite  the political opposition that developed  on both the right and the left of the  political spectrum is he party opposed  it a lot of teacher unions opposed it a  lot of teachers have opposed Common Core  so why I'm going to ask especially about  the teachers why did why did opposition  to Common Core State Standards develop  among teachers I support the principle  of what those standards in body which is  interstate competition you know we have  our own standards in Louisiana called  the Louisiana student standards that  allow us to remain comparable with  states across the country and I support  those standards with respect to teachers  first there is no one teacher voice and  I am sick and tired of people saying  that teachers be viewed as our teachers  believe it that way every year I get in  a room with five or six thousand  teachers in New Orleans for a group of  gathering called our teacher leaders  there are five or 6,000 different views  about a lot of different stuff in there  but there is no one in  that room was just against progress and  I actually think that when you engage  teachers in a conversation you empower  them with curriculum that they actually  know is aligned to the standards they  get fired up about the state standards  in our case Louisiana student standards  there may have been I think some steps  along the way that didn't give teachers  the tools they needed to be confident  when they were doing it when you're cut  not confident you're fearful and when  you're fearful you say it and that  happened a lot in some states including  our own occasionally but I think we've  bridged that cap we've gotten beyond it  and I'll say I don't feel any more  support for high expectations from any  group more than I do from teachers what  I think I'm hearing John is that you're  saying there's been a lot of political  conflict over common core state  standards but this movement toward  setting standards that are closer to  being the same across the state is not  going to go away is there's more and  deeper support we've seen this with our  education next poll that in fact there  is a lot of support for similar  standards across the country and I don't  know if that's just what people say in  response to questions that or but but I  think I hear you're saying that there is  support for that I believe that in a  state that has historically been rated  low that our kids are born just as smart  and just as capable as any kids in this  country I believe they have gifts and  talents that are no lesser than those  are the children in any other state and  I believe the people of Louisiana want  controlled the education system there  but they also support the idea that our  kids can compete and deserve a level  playing field and they shouldn't have to  wait until the AC teen 11th grade to get  it  well we have a new law called the  education oh I can't remember how every  student succeeds Act every student  succeeds Act so how is this affecting  the whole setting of standards and the  setting of accountability that's a big  question and I think it's a big if  states have a lot more control even  districts have a lot more control over  these questions and they did under not  just No Child Left Behind but the  waivers to No Child Left Behind and I  think whether States make a decision  that on some elements they're going to  compete that's still left up in the air  states also now have the  to use measures in their system that  aren't just about tests and how  innovative those things will be how much  of a free pass those things will be and  it goes a lot up in the air on those  questions so we don't know exactly where  we're going  in our educational system as we enter  the latter part of the second decade of  the 21st century there's a lot of  questions yet to be answered but if we  don't improve our schools work how are  we going to be a continually prosperous  country as we move into the 21st century  it would be impossible for us to be and  you know my hope and I do believe that  in many states this will happen is it  will go back to the record and we'll see  that the following formula is a good  formula for success one high  expectations that are measured in a way  where they can be validated across state  lines to an acceptance that our  accountability system does need to  evolve there are higher achieving  systems around the world that have non  tested measures look at New Zealand for  example and look at what they've done  with their system to great effect and  three when schools go off the rails and  we all in every state have these issues  you need to be able to intervene but  we've got a decade of research now to  show what works and what doesn't so if  you are willingness willing to embrace  change willing to embrace high  expectations and follow the research  when there's problems that's a formula  for success I don't see why a state  would go in a different direction from  that and if we do it'll work thank you  John this has been a great opportunity  to get to understand your philosophy and  your strategy for school reform thank  you very much thanks Paul  you
 

